window.docContents[200].resolve({"/Colorings/#section-correct-and-efficient-csimp":{"contents":"In greedyMemo_eq_greedy above we proved that both definitions compute the same\ncolouring: greedy has the cleanest definition and proofs due to Lean's support for\nwell-founded recursion on ℕ and the simplicity of Nat.find, but greedyMemo is\nmuch more efficient. So which should we have chosen to implement?\n\nIt turns out that we don't have to choose! Lean's csimp attribute (\"compiler\nsimp\") registers a proved equation between two functions and makes the\ncompiler use the right-hand side whenever it compiles code that mentions the\nleft-hand side. So by marking the next result with @[csimp], every definition and\nproof below refers to greedy, while every #eval compiled from now on runs the\nefficient memoized implementation.\n\n@[csimp]\ntheorem greedy_eq_greedyMemo : greedy = greedyMemo := by\n  funext G _ n\n  exact (G.greedyMemo_eq_greedy n).symm\n\n\nSuddenly greedy is much faster!\n\n#eval (⊤ : SimpleGraph ℕ).greedy 200 -- 200\n\n\nTo be clear: @[csimp] rewrites greedy in code that is compiled after the\nattribute is registered. Code compiled before it keeps its old, super-exponential\nimplementation. For example greedyColoringNat and greedyColoringFin, whose\ntoFun fields used the original greedy when they were defined, are still slow.\n(This is why the computable colourings of the next section are defined only now, and\ndirectly in terms of our new, efficient version of greedy.)\n\nend evaluating\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Colorings\u0009Greedy Colouring*\u0009Making  greedy  Fast*","header":"7.8.6.3. Correct and Efficient:  csimp","id":"/Colorings/#section-correct-and-efficient-csimp"},"/Connectivity/#section-connected-components":{"contents":"The connected components of G are the equivalence classes of Reachable.\n\nA graph with three connected components\n\n#source ConnectedComponent -- def ConnectedComponent := Quot G.Reachable\n\n\nThe component containing a given vertex.\n\n#check G.connectedComponentMk  -- G.connectedComponentMk : V → G.ConnectedComponent\n\n\nWe introduce the notation G⟦v⟧ for the connected component of G containing the\nvertex v.\n\n@[inherit_doc connectedComponentMk]\nscoped notation:max G \"⟦\" v \"⟧\" => SimpleGraph.connectedComponentMk G v\n\n\nTwo components are equal iff they are constructed from reachable vertices.\n\n#check ConnectedComponent.eq\n\n\nIf we want to prove a fact about all components of a graph then we can use\nConnectedComponent.ind which says that to prove β c holds, for all components\nc, it is sufficient to prove β G⟦v⟧ for all vertices v.\n\n#check ConnectedComponent.ind\n\n\nIf a pair of vertices are adjacent then they are in the same component.\n\n#check ConnectedComponent.connectedComponentMk_eq_of_adj\n\n\nFor a finite graph with decidable adjacency, G.ConnectedComponent is a Fintype,\nso we can count the components. The empty graph ⊥ on Fin 3 has three, while\nC[5] has one.\n\n#eval Fintype.card (⊥ : SimpleGraph (Fin 3)).ConnectedComponent -- 3\n#eval Fintype.card C[5].ConnectedComponent -- 1\n\n\nA connected graph has exactly one component. We state this with Nat.card, so it\napplies to infinite graphs too. Mathlib's\nPreconnected.subsingleton_connectedComponent says that there is at most one\ncomponent, and a connected graph is nonempty, so there is at least one\n(Nat.card_eq_one_iff_unique).\n\nlemma Connected.card_connectedComponent (h : G.Connected) :\n    Nat.card G.ConnectedComponent = 1 :=\n  Nat.card_eq_one_iff_unique.mpr ⟨h.preconnected.subsingleton_connectedComponent,\n    h.nonempty.map G.connectedComponentMk⟩\n\n\nConversely, a graph with exactly one component is connected: any two vertices lie in\nthe same component, and the graph is nonempty since that component has a\nrepresentative vertex (ConnectedComponent.out).\n\nlemma connected_iff_card_connectedComponent :\n    G.Connected ↔ Nat.card G.ConnectedComponent = 1 := by\n  refine ⟨Connected.card_connectedComponent, fun h => ?_⟩\n  obtain ⟨_, ⟨C⟩⟩ := Nat.card_eq_one_iff_unique.mp h\n  have : Nonempty V := ⟨C.out⟩\n  exact ⟨fun u v => ConnectedComponent.exact (Subsingleton.elim _ _)⟩\n\n\nIn informal graph theory we often think of a connected component as a graph, or\nsubgraph, or subset of the vertex set of the original graph. But in Lean a connected\ncomponent C : G.ConnectedComponent is none of these: it is an element of the\nquotient type G.ConnectedComponent of V by the equivalence relation\nReachable, that is, an equivalence class of vertices. However we can convert a\nconnected component into all of these things.\n\nThe support of a component is the set of vertices in that component.\n\n#check ConnectedComponent.supp --  (C : G.ConnectedComponent) : Set V\n\n\nThe SimpleGraph and Subgraph given by a component are C.toSimpleGraph and\nC.toSubgraph.\n\n#source ConnectedComponent.toSimpleGraph -- .. SimpleGraph C := G.induce C.supp\n#source ConnectedComponent.toSubgraph    -- .. G.Subgraph := Subgraph.induce ⊤ C.supp\n\n\nConnectivity of a G.Subgraph is a separate notion, defined via the coercion of the\nsubgraph to a SimpleGraph on its vertex set. This is one of the duplications\ndiscussed in Duplication; we will use it in the proof of Brooks'\ntheorem.\n\nThe subgraph of a connected component is connected.\n\n#source Subgraph.Connected\n#check ConnectedComponent.connected_toSubgraph\n\n\nA set of vertices is maximal with respect to inducing a connected graph iff it is the\nsupport of a component.\n\n#check ConnectedComponent.maximal_connected_induce_iff\n\n\nWhen proving, for example, that a graph is bipartite it is standard to say \"we just\nneed to 2-colour each component\" since if we have 2-colourings for each component\nof a graph we can combine them into a 2-colouring of the whole graph. This is more\ngenerally true for homomorphisms.\n\n#check homOfConnectedComponents\n\n\nRecall our definition of a SimpleGraph.sigma graph: the disjoint union of an\nindexed family of graphs.\n\n#check SimpleGraph.sigma\n\n\nThere is a natural isomorphism between a graph and the sigma graph of its connected\ncomponents, sending a vertex v to the pair ⟨G⟦v⟧, v⟩. Informally this is\nobvious, but in Lean we meet again the cast h ▸ w from the definition of Σg\n(SimpleGraph.sigma): the images of v and w are adjacent\nwhen there is a proof h : G⟦v⟧ = G⟦w⟧ such that v is adjacent to h ▸ w in\nG⟦v⟧.toSimpleGraph. The cast is stuck until h is substituted away, and here\nwe cannot do that: subst needs one side of the equation to be a variable, but\nG⟦v⟧ and G⟦w⟧ are terms.\n\nOne solution is to prove the adjacency rule once, for indices that are variables. A\ncomponent's graph C.toSimpleGraph is by definition the induced graph\nG.induce C.supp, so it is enough to describe adjacency in a sigma graph of induced\ngraphs: ⟨i, u⟩ and ⟨j, v⟩ are adjacent iff i = j and u and v are adjacent\nin G. This statement involves no cast, and in its proof the pattern rfl\nsubstitutes i = j away, since i and j are variables.\n\nlemma sigma_induce_adj {ι : Type*} {S : ι → Set V} {i j : ι} {u : S i} {v : S j} :\n    (Σg fun i => G.induce (S i)).Adj ⟨i, u⟩ ⟨j, v⟩ ↔ i = j ∧ G.Adj u v := by\n  constructor <;> rintro ⟨rfl, h⟩ <;> exact ⟨rfl, h⟩\n\n\nIn the isomorphism the condition G⟦v⟧ = G⟦w⟧ is then automatic, since adjacent\nvertices lie in the same component\n(ConnectedComponent.connectedComponentMk_eq_of_adj). The proof of right_inv uses\nthe same substitution idea: a vertex of the sigma graph is a component C together\nwith a vertex v and a proof of v ∈ C, which by definition says G⟦v⟧ = C, and\nsince C is a variable the pattern rfl substitutes it.\n\nThe isomorphism between G and the sigma graph of its connected components./-- The isomorphism between G and the sigma graph of its connected components. -/\ndef isoSigmaConnectedComponents :\n    G ≃g Σg (fun C : G.ConnectedComponent => C.toSimpleGraph) where\n  toFun v := ⟨G⟦v⟧, ⟨v, rfl⟩⟩\n  invFun p := p.2\n  left_inv v := rfl\n  right_inv := by\n    rintro ⟨_, ⟨v, rfl⟩⟩\n    rfl\n  map_rel_iff' := sigma_induce_adj.trans\n    ⟨And.right, fun h => ⟨ConnectedComponent.connectedComponentMk_eq_of_adj h, h⟩⟩\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Connectivity\u0009Connectivity","header":"10.1.2. Connected Components","id":"/Connectivity/#section-connected-components"},"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-exercises-decidability-finiteness-and-degrees":{"contents":"The first exercises are on finiteness and counting.\n\n1. In the chapter we saw that bad_card_edgeFinset demanded more instances than its\nstatement used. Prove that a graph has at most Fintype.card (Sym2 V) edges,\nassuming only the two instances that the statement itself mentions.\n\nexample [Fintype G.edgeSet] [Fintype (Sym2 V)] :\n    G.edgeFinset.card ≤ Fintype.card (Sym2 V) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n2. Show that the edge set of a graph on a finite vertex type is finite, using\nFinite rather than Fintype. Note that no DecidableRel G.Adj is needed, unlike\nfor Fintype G.edgeSet: why not?\n\nexample [Finite V] : Finite G.edgeSet := by\n  sorry\n\n\n3. The chapter showed G.edgeSet.ncard = G.edgeFinset.card. Prove the ℕ∞ version.\n\nexample [Fintype G.edgeSet] : G.edgeSet.encard = G.edgeFinset.card := by\n  sorry\n\n\n4. The infinite path pathNat₁ has infinitely many edges. Show that the ℕ∞-valued\nencard of its edge set is therefore ⊤, while the ℕ-valued ncard returns the\njunk value 0. Hint: the edges s(n, n + 1) are distinct for distinct n, so\nSet.infinite_of_injective_forall_mem shows that the edge set is infinite.\n\nexample : pathNat₁.edgeSet.encard = ⊤ ∧ pathNat₁.edgeSet.ncard = 0 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n5. The degree of a vertex is the Nat.card of its neighbourhood.\n\nexample (v : V) [Fintype (G.neighborSet v)] :\n    Nat.card (G.neighborSet v) = G.degree v := by\n  sorry\n\n\n6. In the complete graph every vertex is adjacent to every other vertex: its\nneighborFinset is Finset.univ with the vertex itself removed.\n\nexample (n : ℕ) (v : Fin n) : K[n].neighborFinset v = Finset.univ.erase v := by\n  sorry\n\n\n7. The infinite path pathNat₁ has no minimum or maximum degree in the sense of\nminDegree and maxDegree, since its vertex type is infinite. Show instead that 1\nis the least, and 2 the greatest, degree of a vertex of pathNat₁.\n\nexample : IsLeast {d | ∃ v, pathNat₁.degree v = d} 1 ∧\n    IsGreatest {d | ∃ v, pathNat₁.degree v = d} 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n8. A graph on an odd number of vertices that is regular of degree d must have d\neven. (Hint: use IsRegularOfDegree.two_mul_card_edgeFinset. The statement does not\nmention the edge set, so get the Fintype G.edgeSet instance that the proof needs\nfrom classical.)\n\nexample [Fintype V] [G.LocallyFinite] {d : ℕ} (h : G.IsRegularOfDegree d)\n    (hV : Odd (Fintype.card V)) : Even d := by\n  sorry\n\n\n9. Prove that the complete graph on Fin (n + 1) has n * (n + 1) / 2 edges.\n\nexample (n : ℕ) :  K[n + 1].edgeFinset.card = n * (n + 1) / 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n10. Show that in any graph\nG.edgeFinset.card ≤ Fintype.card V * (Fintype.card V - 1) / 2.\n\nexample [Fintype V] [Fintype G.edgeSet] :\n    G.edgeFinset.card ≤ Fintype.card V * (Fintype.card V - 1) / 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n11. Show that k times the number of vertices of degree at least k is at most\ntwice the number of edges. (The set of such vertices is a Finset written with the\nset-builder notation of Set-Builder Notation. Compare it with the\nhandshake lemma.)\n\nexample [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] (k : ℕ) :\n    k * ({v | k ≤ G.degree v} : Finset V).card ≤ 2 * G.edgeFinset.card := by\n  sorry\n\n\n12. The complement of the hypercube Q[n] is regular of degree 2 ^ n - 1 - n.\n(Hint: IsRegularOfDegree.compl takes [DecidableRel G.Adj], so it needs Q[n] to\nbe regular with respect to the filtering instance for its neighbourhoods; see\nStating Results Across a Diamond.)\n\nexample (n : ℕ) : Q[n]ᶜ.IsRegularOfDegree (2 ^ n - 1 - n) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n13. We saw that the n-uniform blow-up of a locally finite graph is locally finite.\nIn this exercise we generalize this to the case when each of the classes in the\nblow-up is finite (but they may have different sizes). We make this a def rather\nthan an instance: since G[n] is G.blowUpGraph (fun _ => Fin n), an instance\nwould give a second route to Fintype (G[n].neighborSet w), alongside the instance\nfrom the chapter, creating a diamond.\n\n@[instance_reducible]\ndef locallyFiniteBlowUpGraph' {ι : Type*} (H : SimpleGraph ι) [H.LocallyFinite]\n    (V : ι → Type*) [∀ i, Fintype (V i)] : LocallyFinite (H.blowUpGraph V) :=\n  fun ⟨i, v⟩ => Fintype.ofEquiv (Σ k : H.neighborSet i, V k)\n    -- the goal is to show the obvious Equiv between neighbourhoods\n    sorry\n\n\n14. Prove that if V is finite then the n-th iterated Mycielskian has\n2 ^ n * Fintype.card V + 2 ^ n - 1 vertices.\n\nlemma card_mycielskianVert' (n : ℕ) [Fintype V] :\n    Fintype.card (mμᵥ^n V) = 2 ^ n * Fintype.card V + 2 ^ n - 1 := by\n  sorry\n\n\nThe next few exercises are on the degrees of vertices in the Mycielskian.\n\nAs in Mycielskian Degrees, statements about degrees and edge sets\ntake the Fintype instances they mention as arguments. Those about δ, Δ and\ndegreeMultiset take [Fintype V] and [DecidableRel G.Adj] instead, since these\ndefinitions build their own instances from them. Use the classical tactic for\nany further instances a proof needs. (We also open Finset since we will need quite\na few lemmas about Finset.)\n\n15. The degree of the new vertex none in the Mycielskian is the number of vertices\nin the original graph.\n\nopen Finset\n\n\nlemma degree_mycielskian_none' [Fintype V] [Fintype (mμ[G].neighborSet none)] :\n    mμ[G].degree none = Fintype.card V := by\n  sorry\n\n\n16. The degree of the right copy of a vertex in the Mycielskian is one more than the\ndegree of the vertex in the original graph.\n\nlemma degree_mycielskian_right' (v : V) [Fintype (G.neighborSet v)]\n    [Fintype (mμ[G].neighborSet (v ᵣ))] :\n    mμ[G].degree v ᵣ = G.degree v + 1 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n17. The number of edges in mμ[G] is 3 * G.edgeFinset.card + Fintype.card V.\n(Use the version of the handshake lemma that takes its instances as arguments.)\n\nlemma card_edgeFinset_mycielskian' [Fintype V] [Fintype G.edgeSet]\n    [Fintype mμ[G].edgeSet] :\n     #mμ[G].edgeFinset = 3 * # G.edgeFinset + Fintype.card V := by\n  sorry\n\n\n18. The maximum degree of mμ[G] is the maximum of 2 * Δ(G) and the number of\nvertices in G.\n\nlemma maxDegree_mycielskian' [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n    Δ(mμ[G]) = max (2 * Δ(G)) (Fintype.card V) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n19. The minimum degree of mμ[G] is the minimum of 2 * δ(G) and δ(G) + 1.\nDeduce that δ(mμ[G]) = δ(G) + 1 whenever δ(G) ≥ 1.\n\nlemma minDegree_mycielskian' [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n    δ(mμ[G]) = min (2 * δ(G)) (δ(G) + 1) := by\n  sorry\n\n\nlemma minDegree_mycielskian_of_one_le' [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    (h : 1 ≤ δ(G)) : δ(mμ[G]) = δ(G) + 1 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n20. The degreeMultiset of the complete graph K[n] is the multiset of n copies\nof n - 1.\n\ntheorem degreeMultiset_top' (n : ℕ) :\n    K[n].degreeMultiset = Multiset.replicate n (n - 1) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n21. A multiset of n copies of n - 1 is graphic, realized by the complete graph\nK[n].\n\ntheorem graphic_const' (n : ℕ) :\n    Graphic (Multiset.replicate n (n - 1)) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n22. Every graphic multiset has an even sum. (This shows that the parity condition in\nthe Erdős–Gallai theorem is necessary.)\n\ntheorem Graphic.even_sum' {m : Multiset ℕ} (h : Graphic m) : Even m.sum := by\n  sorry\n\n\n23. In the chapter we computed the degree of the new vertex of coneGraph H s. Show\nthat an old vertex gains one on its degree exactly when it lies in s.\n\nlemma degree_coneGraph_some' [DecidableEq V] (H : SimpleGraph V) (s : Finset V)\n    (w : V) [Fintype (H.neighborSet w)]\n    [Fintype ((coneGraph H s).neighborSet (some w))] :\n    (coneGraph H s).degree (some w) = H.degree w + (if w ∈ s then 1 else 0) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n24. The degreeMultiset of the cone: the new vertex contributes #s, and every old\nvertex has its old degree plus one when it lies in s. (Hint: the stub below records\nthat Finset.univ on Option V is none followed by the image of Finset.univ\nunder some. This holds by rfl.)\n\ntheorem degreeMultiset_coneGraph' [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] (H : SimpleGraph V)\n    [DecidableRel H.Adj] (s : Finset V) :\n    (coneGraph H s).degreeMultiset = #s ::ₘ (Finset.univ : Finset V).val.map\n      (fun w => H.degree w + if w ∈ s then 1 else 0) := by\n  have h : (Finset.univ : Finset (Option V)).val =\n      none ::ₘ (Finset.univ : Finset V).val.map some := rfl\n  sorry\n\n\n25. The degreeMultiset of mμ[G] is the multiset of degrees\nof G with each degree doubled, together with the degrees of the new vertices.\n(Hint: the stub below records how Finset.univ unfolds on Option α and on\nα ⊕ β. Both facts hold by rfl, as in Exercise 24.)\n\nlemma degreeMultiset_mycielskian' [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n    mμ[G].degreeMultiset = {Fintype.card V} + (G.degreeMultiset.map (2 * · ))\n                           + (G.degreeMultiset.map (· + 1)) := by\n  simp only [degreeMultiset]\n  have h1 : (Finset.univ : Finset (Option (V ⊕ V))).val =\n          none ::ₘ Multiset.map Option.some (Finset.univ : Finset (V ⊕ V)).val := rfl\n  have h2 : (Finset.univ : Finset (V ⊕ V)).val = Multiset.map Sum.inl\n                Finset.univ.val + Multiset.map Sum.inr Finset.univ.val := rfl\n  sorry\n\n\n26. In a locally finite graph G, there is a vertex of degree at least 3 iff\n ∃ a b c d, G.Adj a b ∧ G.Adj a c ∧ G.Adj a d ∧ b ≠ c ∧ b ≠ d ∧ c ≠ d\n\nlemma exists_three_le_degree_iff' {V : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V)\n    [G.LocallyFinite] : (∃ v, 3 ≤ G.degree v) ↔\n      ∃ a b c d, G.Adj a b ∧ G.Adj a c ∧ G.Adj a d ∧ b ≠ c ∧ b ≠ d ∧ c ≠ d := by\n  sorry\n\n\nThe final exercises return to decidability, and give decidable adjacency instances\nfor the line graph and the hasse graph, both of which Mathlib is missing.\n\n27. Give a decidable adjacency instance for the line graph L[G] of a graph on a\nvertex type with decidable equality. Recall that the vertices of L[G] are the edges\nof G, and that two of them are adjacent iff they are distinct and share a vertex\n(lineGraph_adj_iff_exists).\n\n(Hint: to decide whether two edges e₁ e₂ : Sym2 V share a vertex, use\nSym2.recOnSubsingleton to write them as s(a, b) and s(c, d), and reduce to the\nfour equalities a = c, a = d, b = c and b = d with decidable_of_iff.\nRecursion on Sym2 is only allowed when the result cannot depend on the order of the\ntwo endpoints; recOnSubsingleton ensures this by requiring the result to lie in a\nSubsingleton (a type with at most one element), which Decidable p is.)\n\nexample {V : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableEq V] : DecidableRel L[G].Adj :=\n  sorry\n\n\n28. In a locally finite order a ⋖ b holds iff a < b and the interval\nFinset.Ioo a b is empty, so the covering relation is decidable. Give the instance,\nbuilding it with decidable_of_iff rather than rw so that decide can still\nreduce it.\n\nexample {α : Type*} [Preorder α] [DecidableEq α] [DecidableLT α]\n    [LocallyFiniteOrder α] : DecidableRel ((· ⋖ ·) : α → α → Prop) :=\n  sorry\n\n\n29. Show that the hasse graph of a preorder with a decidable covering relation has\ndecidable adjacency.\n\nexample (α : Type*) [Preorder α] [DecidableRel ((· ⋖ ·) : α → α → Prop)] :\n    DecidableRel (hasse α).Adj :=\n  sorry\n\n\n1. In the chapter we saw that bad_card_edgeFinset demanded more instances than its\nstatement used. Prove that a graph has at most Fintype.card (Sym2 V) edges,\nassuming only the two instances that the statement itself mentions.example [Fintype G.edgeSet] [Fintype (Sym2 V)] :\n    G.edgeFinset.card ≤ Fintype.card (Sym2 V) :=\n  Finset.card_le_univ _\nAssuming [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] would restrict the lemma to callers\nwho obtain Fintype G.edgeSet by that particular route, exactly as for\nbad_card_edgeFinset.2. Show that the edge set of a graph on a finite vertex type is finite, using\nFinite rather than Fintype. Note that no DecidableRel G.Adj is needed, unlike\nfor Fintype G.edgeSet: why not?example [Finite V] : Finite G.edgeSet :=\n  -- Sym2 V is finite since V is (it is a quotient of V × V: Quot.finite),\n  -- and a subtype of a finite type is finite (Subtype.finite). Being a\n  -- proposition, Finite carries no data and so has nothing to compute; the\n  -- decidability was only ever needed to *filter* Finset.univ down to the edges.\n  inferInstance\n3. The chapter showed G.edgeSet.ncard = G.edgeFinset.card. Prove the ℕ∞ version.example [Fintype G.edgeSet] : G.edgeSet.encard = G.edgeFinset.card := by\n  rw [← coe_edgeFinset, Set.encard_coe_eq_coe_finsetCard]\n4. The infinite path pathNat₁ has infinitely many edges. Show that the ℕ∞-valued\nencard of its edge set is therefore ⊤, while the ℕ-valued ncard returns the\njunk value 0. Hint: the edges s(n, n + 1) are distinct for distinct n, so\nSet.infinite_of_injective_forall_mem shows that the edge set is infinite.example : pathNat₁.edgeSet.encard = ⊤ ∧ pathNat₁.edgeSet.ncard = 0 := by\n  have h : pathNat₁.edgeSet.Infinite :=\n    Set.infinite_of_injective_forall_mem (f := fun n : ℕ => s(n, n + 1))\n      (fun a b hab => by grind [Sym2.eq_iff]) (fun n => by simp)\n  exact ⟨h.encard_eq, h.ncard⟩\n5. The degree of a vertex is the Nat.card of its neighbourhood.example (v : V) [Fintype (G.neighborSet v)] :\n    Nat.card (G.neighborSet v) = G.degree v := by\n  rw [Nat.card_eq_fintype_card, card_neighborSet_eq_degree]\n6. In the complete graph every vertex is adjacent to every other vertex: its\nneighborFinset is Finset.univ with the vertex itself removed.example (n : ℕ) (v : Fin n) : K[n].neighborFinset v = Finset.univ.erase v := by\n  ext w; simp [ne_comm]\n7. The infinite path pathNat₁ has no minimum or maximum degree in the sense of\nminDegree and maxDegree, since its vertex type is infinite. Show instead that 1\nis the least, and 2 the greatest, degree of a vertex of pathNat₁.example : IsLeast {d | ∃ v, pathNat₁.degree v = d} 1 ∧\n    IsGreatest {d | ∃ v, pathNat₁.degree v = d} 2 := by\n  -- the degrees 1 and 2 are attained at the vertices 0 and 1\n  refine ⟨⟨⟨0, by simp⟩, ?_⟩, ⟨⟨1, by simp⟩, ?_⟩⟩\n  all_goals\n    rintro _ ⟨v, rfl⟩\n    simp only [degree_pathNat₁]\n    split_ifs <;> simp\n8. A graph on an odd number of vertices that is regular of degree d must have d\neven. (Hint: use IsRegularOfDegree.two_mul_card_edgeFinset. The statement does not\nmention the edge set, so get the Fintype G.edgeSet instance that the proof needs\nfrom classical.)example [Fintype V] [G.LocallyFinite] {d : ℕ} (h : G.IsRegularOfDegree d)\n    (hV : Odd (Fintype.card V)) : Even d := by\n  classical\n  have h2 : Even (Fintype.card V * d) := h.two_mul_card_edgeFinset ▸ even_two_mul _\n  exact (Nat.even_mul.1 h2).resolve_left (Nat.not_even_iff_odd.2 hV)\n9. Prove that the complete graph on Fin (n + 1) has n * (n + 1) / 2 edges.example (n : ℕ) :  K[n + 1].edgeFinset.card = n * (n + 1) / 2 := by\n  rw [card_edgeFinset_top_eq_card_choose_two, Fintype.card_fin, Nat.choose_two_right,\n      Nat.add_sub_cancel_right, mul_comm]\n10. Show that in any graph\nG.edgeFinset.card ≤ Fintype.card V * (Fintype.card V - 1) / 2.example [Fintype V] [Fintype G.edgeSet] :\n    G.edgeFinset.card ≤ Fintype.card V * (Fintype.card V - 1) / 2 :=\n  G.card_edgeFinset_le_card_choose_two.trans (Nat.choose_two_right _).le\n11. Show that k times the number of vertices of degree at least k is at most\ntwice the number of edges. (The set of such vertices is a Finset written with the\nset-builder notation of Set-Builder Notation. Compare it with the\nhandshake lemma.)example [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] (k : ℕ) :\n    k * ({v | k ≤ G.degree v} : Finset V).card ≤ 2 * G.edgeFinset.card := by\n  rw [← sum_degrees_eq_twice_card_edges]\n  calc k * ({v | k ≤ G.degree v} : Finset V).card\n      = ∑ v ∈ ({v | k ≤ G.degree v} : Finset V), k := by simp [mul_comm]\n    _ ≤ ∑ v ∈ ({v | k ≤ G.degree v} : Finset V), G.degree v :=\n        Finset.sum_le_sum fun v hv => by simpa using hv\n    _ ≤ ∑ v, G.degree v := Finset.sum_le_sum_of_subset (Finset.subset_univ _)\n12. The complement of the hypercube Q[n] is regular of degree 2 ^ n - 1 - n.\n(Hint: IsRegularOfDegree.compl takes [DecidableRel G.Adj], so it needs Q[n] to\nbe regular with respect to the filtering instance for its neighbourhoods; see\nStating Results Across a Diamond.)example (n : ℕ) : Q[n]ᶜ.IsRegularOfDegree (2 ^ n - 1 - n) := by\n  -- put the filtering instance in scope, so that hypercube_isRegularOfDegree\n  -- produces the regularity statement IsRegularOfDegree.compl expects\n  let : Q[n].LocallyFinite := fun v => Subtype.fintype _\n  have := (hypercube_isRegularOfDegree n).compl\n  rwa [card_hypercubeVert] at this\n13. We saw that the n-uniform blow-up of a locally finite graph is locally finite.\nIn this exercise we generalize this to the case when each of the classes in the\nblow-up is finite (but they may have different sizes). We make this a def rather\nthan an instance: since G[n] is G.blowUpGraph (fun _ => Fin n), an instance\nwould give a second route to Fintype (G[n].neighborSet w), alongside the instance\nfrom the chapter, creating a diamond.A blow-up of a locally finite graph with finite classes is locally finite./-- A blow-up of a locally finite graph with finite classes is locally finite. -/\n@[instance_reducible]\ndef locallyFiniteBlowUpGraph {ι : Type*} (H : SimpleGraph ι) [H.LocallyFinite]\n    (V : ι → Type*) [∀ i, Fintype (V i)] : LocallyFinite (H.blowUpGraph V) :=\n  fun ⟨i, v⟩ => Fintype.ofEquiv (Σ k : H.neighborSet i, V k)\n    { toFun := fun ⟨⟨k, hk⟩, y⟩ => ⟨⟨k, y⟩, hk⟩\n      invFun := fun ⟨⟨k, y⟩, hadj⟩ => ⟨⟨k, hadj⟩, y⟩}\n14. Prove that if V is finite then the n-th iterated Mycielskian has\n2 ^ n * Fintype.card V + 2 ^ n - 1 vertices.lemma card_mycielskianVert (n : ℕ) [Fintype V] :\n    Fintype.card (mμᵥ^n V) = 2 ^ n * Fintype.card V + 2 ^ n - 1 := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero => unfold MycielskianVert; grind\n  | succ n ih =>\n    unfold MycielskianVert\n    grind [card_mycielskianVert_one, Nat.mul_sub_left_distrib, mul_add,\n            ← mul_assoc, ← pow_succ']\n15. The degree of the new vertex none in the Mycielskian is the number of vertices\nin the original graph.@[simp]\nlemma degree_mycielskian_none [Fintype V] [Fintype (mμ[G].neighborSet none)] :\n    mμ[G].degree none = Fintype.card V := by\n  classical\n  have hnb : mμ[G].neighborFinset none = (univ : Finset V).image (· ᵣ) := by\n    ext x; simp [← mem_neighborSet]\n  rw [← card_neighborFinset_eq_degree, hnb,\n    card_image_of_injective _ mycielskian.right_injective, card_univ]\n16. The degree of the right copy of a vertex in the Mycielskian is one more than the\ndegree of the vertex in the original graph.@[simp]\nlemma degree_mycielskian_right (v : V) [Fintype (G.neighborSet v)]\n    [Fintype (mμ[G].neighborSet (v ᵣ))] :\n    mμ[G].degree v ᵣ = G.degree v + 1 := by\n  classical\n  have hnb :\n      mμ[G].neighborFinset (v ᵣ) = (G.neighborFinset v).image (· ₗ) ∪ {none} := by\n    ext x; simp [← mem_neighborSet]\n  rw [← card_neighborFinset_eq_degree, ← card_neighborFinset_eq_degree, hnb,\n    card_union_of_disjoint (by simp [← Finset.disjoint_coe]),\n    card_image_of_injective _ mycielskian.left_injective, card_singleton]\n17. The number of edges in mμ[G] is 3 * G.edgeFinset.card + Fintype.card V.\n(Use the version of the handshake lemma that takes its instances as arguments.)@[simp]\nlemma card_edgeFinset_mycielskian [Fintype V] [Fintype G.edgeSet]\n    [Fintype mμ[G].edgeSet] :\n     #mμ[G].edgeFinset = 3 * # G.edgeFinset + Fintype.card V := by\n  classical\n  apply (Nat.mul_right_inj (show 2 ≠ 0 by decide)).1\n  simp_rw [← sum_degrees_eq_twice_card_edges' mμ[G], Fintype.sum_option,\n    degree_mycielskian_none, Fintype.sum_sum_type, degree_mycielskian_left,\n    ← mul_sum, degree_mycielskian_right, sum_add_distrib,\n    sum_degrees_eq_twice_card_edges' G, sum_const, card_univ, smul_eq_mul, mul_one]\n  grind\n18. The maximum degree of mμ[G] is the maximum of 2 * Δ(G) and the number of\nvertices in G.lemma maxDegree_mycielskian [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n    Δ(mμ[G]) = max (2 * Δ(G)) (Fintype.card V) := by\n  apply le_antisymm\n  · apply maxDegree_le_of_forall_degree_le\n    intro v\n    rcases v with none | (v | v)\n    · simp\n    · rw [degree_mycielskian_left]\n      exact (Nat.mul_le_mul_left 2 (G.degree_le_maxDegree v)).trans (le_max_left _ _)\n    · rw [degree_mycielskian_right]\n      exact (Nat.succ_le_of_lt (G.degree_lt_card_verts v)).trans (le_max_right _ _)\n  · cases isEmpty_or_nonempty V\n    · simp\n    · apply max_le\n      · obtain ⟨v, hv⟩ := G.exists_maximal_degree_vertex\n        rw [hv, ← degree_mycielskian_left]\n        exact mμ[G].degree_le_maxDegree (v ₗ)\n      · rw [← G.degree_mycielskian_none]\n        exact mμ[G].degree_le_maxDegree none\n19. The minimum degree of mμ[G] is the minimum of 2 * δ(G) and δ(G) + 1.\nDeduce that δ(mμ[G]) = δ(G) + 1 whenever δ(G) ≥ 1.lemma minDegree_mycielskian [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n    δ(mμ[G]) = min (2 * δ(G)) (δ(G) + 1) := by\n  obtain hV | hV := isEmpty_or_nonempty V\n  · simp [minDegree]\n  apply le_antisymm\n  · obtain ⟨v, hv⟩ := G.exists_minimal_degree_vertex\n    apply le_min\n    · rw [hv, ← degree_mycielskian_left]\n      exact mμ[G].minDegree_le_degree (v ₗ)\n    · rw [hv, ← degree_mycielskian_right]\n      exact mμ[G].minDegree_le_degree (v ᵣ)\n  · apply le_minDegree_of_forall_le_degree\n    intro v\n    rcases v with none | (v | v)\n    · rw [degree_mycielskian_none]\n      exact (min_le_right _ _).trans (Nat.succ_le_of_lt G.minDegree_lt_card)\n    · rw [degree_mycielskian_left]\n      exact (min_le_left _ _).trans (Nat.mul_le_mul_left 2 (G.minDegree_le_degree v))\n    · rw [degree_mycielskian_right]\n      -- min_le_right .. is min_le_right _ _: .. fills in all the remaining\n      -- explicit arguments with _\n      exact (min_le_right ..).trans\n        (Nat.add_le_add_right (G.minDegree_le_degree v) 1)\nlemma minDegree_mycielskian_of_one_le [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    (h : 1 ≤ δ(G)) : δ(mμ[G]) = δ(G) + 1 := by\n  rw [minDegree_mycielskian]\n  grind\n20. The degreeMultiset of the complete graph K[n] is the multiset of n copies\nof n - 1.theorem degreeMultiset_top (n : ℕ) :\n    K[n].degreeMultiset = Multiset.replicate n (n - 1) := by\n  simp [degreeMultiset]\n21. A multiset of n copies of n - 1 is graphic, realized by the complete graph\nK[n].theorem graphic_const (n : ℕ) :\n    Graphic (Multiset.replicate n (n - 1)) :=\n  degreeMultiset_top n ▸ graphic_degreeMultiset K[n]\n22. Every graphic multiset has an even sum. (This shows that the parity condition in\nthe Erdős–Gallai theorem is necessary.)theorem Graphic.even_sum {m : Multiset ℕ} (h : Graphic m) : Even m.sum := by\n  obtain ⟨V, _, G, _, rfl⟩ := h\n  -- the sum of the degree multiset is, by definition, the sum of the degrees\n  change Even (∑ v, G.degree v)\n  rw [sum_degrees_eq_twice_card_edges]\n  exact even_two_mul _\n23. In the chapter we computed the degree of the new vertex of coneGraph H s. Show\nthat an old vertex gains one on its degree exactly when it lies in s.@[simp]\ntheorem degree_coneGraph_some [DecidableEq V] (H : SimpleGraph V) (s : Finset V)\n    (w : V) [Fintype (H.neighborSet w)]\n    [Fintype ((coneGraph H s).neighborSet (some w))] :\n    (coneGraph H s).degree (some w) = H.degree w + (if w ∈ s then 1 else 0) := by\n  rw [← card_neighborFinset_eq_degree]\n  have hset : (coneGraph H s).neighborFinset (some w) =\n     (H.neighborFinset w).map Function.Embedding.some ∪\n       (if w ∈ s then {none} else ∅) := by\n    ext x\n    cases x <;> by_cases hw : w ∈ s <;> simp [hw]\n  rw [hset]\n  split_ifs with h <;> simp\n24. The degreeMultiset of the cone: the new vertex contributes #s, and every old\nvertex has its old degree plus one when it lies in s.theorem degreeMultiset_coneGraph [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] (H : SimpleGraph V)\n    [DecidableRel H.Adj] (s : Finset V) :\n    (coneGraph H s).degreeMultiset = #s ::ₘ (Finset.univ : Finset V).val.map\n      (fun w => H.degree w + if w ∈ s then 1 else 0) := by\n  unfold degreeMultiset\n  -- Finset.univ on Option V is by definition none followed by the image of\n  -- Finset.univ under some\n  change (none ::ₘ (Finset.univ : Finset V).val.map some).map _ = _\n  simp\n25. The degreeMultiset of mμ[G] is the multiset of degrees\nof G with each degree doubled, together with the degrees of the new vertices.\n(Hint: the stub below records how Finset.univ unfolds on Option α and on\nα ⊕ β. Both facts hold by rfl, as in Exercise 24.)lemma degreeMultiset_mycielskian [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n    mμ[G].degreeMultiset = {Fintype.card V} + (G.degreeMultiset.map (2 * · ))\n                           + (G.degreeMultiset.map (· + 1)) := by\n  simp only [degreeMultiset]\n  -- Split the universe of Option (V ⊕ V) into the new vertex none, the left\n  -- copies and the right copies.\n  have h1 : (Finset.univ : Finset (Option (V ⊕ V))).val =\n          none ::ₘ Multiset.map Option.some (Finset.univ : Finset (V ⊕ V)).val := rfl\n  have h2 : (Finset.univ : Finset (V ⊕ V)).val = Multiset.map Sum.inl\n                Finset.univ.val + Multiset.map Sum.inr Finset.univ.val := rfl\n  rw [h1, h2]\n  simp only [Multiset.map_cons, Multiset.map_add, Multiset.map_map, Function.comp,\n    degree_mycielskian_none, degree_mycielskian_left, degree_mycielskian_right]\n  rw [← Multiset.singleton_add]\n  abel\n26. In a locally finite graph G, there is a vertex of degree at least 3 iff\n ∃ a b c d, G.Adj a b ∧ G.Adj a c ∧ G.Adj a d ∧ b ≠ c ∧ b ≠ d ∧ c ≠ dlemma exists_three_le_degree_iff {V : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V)\n    [G.LocallyFinite] : (∃ v, 3 ≤ G.degree v) ↔\n      ∃ a b c d, G.Adj a b ∧ G.Adj a c ∧ G.Adj a d ∧ b ≠ c ∧ b ≠ d ∧ c ≠ d := by\n  simp only [Nat.succ_le_iff, ← card_neighborFinset_eq_degree,\n            Finset.two_lt_card_iff, mem_neighborFinset]\nThe final exercises return to decidability, and give decidable adjacency instances\nfor the line graph and the hasse graph, both of which Mathlib is missing.27. Give a decidable adjacency instance for the line graph L[G] of a graph on a\nvertex type with decidable equality. Recall that the vertices of L[G] are the edges\nof G, and that two of them are adjacent iff they are distinct and share a vertex\n(lineGraph_adj_iff_exists).(Hint: to decide whether two edges e₁ e₂ : Sym2 V share a vertex, use\nSym2.recOnSubsingleton to write them as s(a, b) and s(c, d), and reduce to the\nfour equalities a = c, a = d, b = c and b = d with decidable_of_iff.\nRecursion on Sym2 is only allowed when the result cannot depend on the order of the\ntwo endpoints; recOnSubsingleton ensures this by requiring the result to lie in a\nSubsingleton (a type with at most one element), which Decidable p is.)Decidability of the adjacency relation for the line graph./-- Decidability of the adjacency relation for the line graph. -/\ninstance {V : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableEq V] :\n    DecidableRel L[G].Adj := by\n  intro e₁ e₂\n  -- Two edges share a vertex iff one of the four endpoint pairs is equal\n  have : Decidable (∃ v ∈ (e₁ : Sym2 V), v ∈ (e₂ : Sym2 V)) :=\n    Sym2.recOnSubsingleton (e₁ : Sym2 V) fun a b =>\n      Sym2.recOnSubsingleton (e₂ : Sym2 V) fun c d =>\n        decidable_of_iff (a = c ∨ a = d ∨ b = c ∨ b = d) (by aesop)\n  exact decidable_of_iff' _ SimpleGraph.lineGraph_adj_iff_exists\nexample : L[C[5]].Adj ⟨s(0, 1), by decide⟩ ⟨s(1, 2), by decide⟩ := by decide\n28. In a locally finite order a ⋖ b holds iff a < b and the interval\nFinset.Ioo a b is empty, so the covering relation is decidable. Give the instance,\nbuilding it with decidable_of_iff rather than rw so that decide can still\nreduce it.Note that _root_. puts the instance at CovBy.decidableRel, outside the\nSimpleGraph namespace that we are working in, since it is about orders rather than\ngraphs.Decidability of the covering relation of a locally finite order./-- Decidability of the covering relation of a locally finite order. -/\ninstance _root_.CovBy.decidableRel {α : Type*} [Preorder α] [DecidableEq α]\n    [DecidableLT α] [LocallyFiniteOrder α] :\n    DecidableRel ((· ⋖ ·) : α → α → Prop) := fun a b =>\n  decidable_of_iff (a < b ∧ Finset.Ioo a b = ∅)\n    (by simp [covBy_iff_Ioo_eq, ← Finset.coe_Ioo])\n29. Show that the hasse graph of a preorder with a decidable covering relation has\ndecidable adjacency.Decidability of the adjacency relation for the Hasse graph./-- Decidability of the adjacency relation for the Hasse graph. -/\ninstance hasseAdjDecidable (α : Type*) [Preorder α]\n    [DecidableRel ((· ⋖ ·) : α → α → Prop)] :\n    DecidableRel (hasse α).Adj := fun u v =>\n  -- hasse is a def, so we tell Lean what its adjacency unfolds to; the CovBy\n  -- instance assumed above then does the rest.\n  inferInstanceAs (Decidable (u ⋖ v ∨ v ⋖ u))\n\n\nend SimpleGraph\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees","header":"4.7. Exercises","id":"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-exercises-decidability-finiteness-and-degrees"},"/Theorems/#section-andr-sfai-erd-s-s-s-theorem":{"contents":"Turán's theorem says that the densest (r + 1)-clique-free graphs are Turán graphs,\nwhile Füredi's stability theorem tells us that graphs that are almost extremal, in\nterms of number of edges, are almost r-colourable.\n\nThe Andrásfai–Erdős–Sós theorem gives a different type of stability result related to\nthe minimum degree: any (r + 1)-clique-free graph with minimum degree greater than\n(3 * r - 4) * n / (3 * r - 1) must be r-colourable. For r = 2 this says that a\ntriangle-free graph with minimum degree greater than 2 * n / 5 is bipartite. The\nuniform blow-up C[5][t] shows that 2 * n / 5 cannot be lowered (see the\nexercises).\n\nThe 3-uniform blow-up of C_5: a triangle-free graph on n = 15 vertices with minimum degree 6 = 2n/5 that is not bipartite\n\n#check SimpleGraph.colorable_of_cliqueFree_lt_minDegree\n\n\nFor example, if G is triangle-free and has an edge, then mμ[G] is triangle-free\n(CliqueFree.mycielskian) but not 2-colourable, since mμ[G] is\n(k + 1)-colourable only when G is k-colourable\n(colorable_mycielskian_succ_iff). So the Andrásfai–Erdős–Sós theorem forces mμ[G]\nto have minimum degree at most 2 * n / 5, where n = 2 * |V| + 1 is its number of\nvertices.\n\nexample [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] (h3 : G.CliqueFree 3) {u v : V}\n    (huv : G.Adj u v) : δ(mμ[G]) ≤ 2 * (2 * Fintype.card V + 1) / 5 := by\n  by_contra! h\n  have h2 : mμ[G].Colorable 2 := colorable_of_cliqueFree_lt_minDegree h3.mycielskian\n      (by simpa [Fintype.card_option, Fintype.card_sum, two_mul] using h)\n  obtain ⟨c⟩ := (colorable_mycielskian_succ_iff 1).1 h2\n  exact c.valid huv (Subsingleton.elim _ _)\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Theorems\u0009Turán's Theorem, Stability and Asymptotics","header":"13.2.5. Andrásfai–Erdős–Sós Theorem","id":"/Theorems/#section-andr-sfai-erd-s-s-s-theorem"}});